WEBVTT

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:29.960
On August 18, 1934, 20-year-old Ann Cooper Hewitt, heiress to one of the largest fortunes in the United States, was admitted to a San Francisco hospital.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.740
hospital for an emergency appendectomy.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:39.770
She later learned the surgeons not only had removed her appendix, but also a length of

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:44.940
her fallopian tubes, rendering her incapable of ever becoming pregnant.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:54.540
The story of the sterilized heiress hit the papers just after the new year, in 1936, when

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:59.540
Ann filed a half-million-dollar damage claim against the surgeons and her own mother.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:03.790
for sterilizing her without her knowledge or consent.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:07.220
Anne's mother denied any wrongdoing.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:11.480
She'd done what she'd done for society's sake, she insisted,

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:14.190
because her daughter was feeble-minded.

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:18.760
It was the sort of bizarre high society scandal

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.380
that would have captured the national imagination

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:22.940
under any circumstances.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.250
But that one word, feeble-minded,

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:29.060
struck a familiar chord for Americans.

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:37.720
and linked Ann's plight to a decades-old campaign to control human reproduction, known as eugenics.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.560
What is the bearing of the laws of heredity upon human affairs?

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:44.200
Eugenics provides the answer.

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:50.450
Eugenics was proposed as the scientific solution for social problems.

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:59.980
It was a combination of hope and aspiration on one side, and on the other side it was about fear.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.040
in some cases about hate.

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:06.320
They are identified early, categorized,

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:09.600
feeble-minded, imbecile, idiot.

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:11.640
It would have been better by far

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:12.820
if they had never been born.

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:21.860
People tend to think that eugenics was a doctrine

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:23.360
that originated with the Nazis,

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:26.880
that it was grounded in wild claims

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.810
that were far outside the scientific mainstream.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.100
Both of those impressions are fundamentally not true.

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:38.520
It was almost a mania that sort of swept through the country,

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:42.400
and there was that kind of naive, optimistic vision of eugenics,

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:45.600
like, hey, let's all get together and make better people.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:51.370
The eugenics movement was about having healthy children,

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:55.760
about having a stronger society.

00:02:56.830 --> 00:02:58.190
There's nothing wrong with that.

00:02:59.180 --> 00:02:59.980
You have to look...

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.740
at the underbelly of what was implemented in the name of eugenics to see what was so problematic

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:18.340
about it at liberty mutual we believe progress happens when people feel secure that's why we

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:24.160
exist to help people embrace today and pursue tomorrow liberty liberty liberty

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:27.640
liberty mutual insurance is a proud sponsor of american experience

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:59.450
Fall of 1902

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:03.390
an American biologist named Charles Benedict Davenport

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:06.380
arrived in London on a sort of pilgrimage.

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:10.400
He was 36, Harvard-educated,

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:13.170
and like many biologists of his generation,

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:15.960
absorbed with the study of evolution.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:20.610
He'd been traveling in Europe with his wife,

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:24.160
collecting seashells for research on species variation.

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:27.080
But this was to be the highlight of the trip,

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:29.980
a meeting with the world-renowned...

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:39.630
scientist Sir Francis Galton. A pioneering statistician, Galton had lived his 80 years

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:48.080
by a single motto, whenever you can, count. His obsession with measurements and patterns

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:53.980
had led him to create the world's first weather maps, establish fingerprinting as a means of

00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:59.650
identification, and set data-backed parameters for the perfect cup of tea.

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:06.300
Charles Davenport had come to discuss another matter,

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:09.500
Galton's work on heredity.

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:14.170
Francis Galton was a great quantifier.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:17.580
He liked to quantify height, hair color,

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:20.100
you know, what is the chest size of an average man,

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:22.750
what is the thigh length of an average man,

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:25.130
even things like intelligence.

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:29.860
Galton had a theory that...

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.400
Talent, as he called it, what we would call intelligence, seemed to run in families.

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:40.090
And so it quickly occurred to him, if we can get people with high talent to mate with each other,

00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:43.370
prevent people with low talent from mating with each other,

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:47.210
we will, within a few generations, create this race of supermen.

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:57.610
Francis Galton was borrowing ideas and kind of riffing off of the work of his half-cousin, Charles Darwin.

00:05:59.040 --> 00:05:59.980
Darwin believed...

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.940
that evolution was this natural process

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:05.560
that was inevitably leading towards what they call

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:07.000
the survival of the fittest.

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:11.690
Galton really turns that idea on its head and says,

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:15.620
you know, natural selection isn't working very well.

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:18.390
We need to do a form of selection.

00:06:18.970 --> 00:06:19.890
We need to intervene.

00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:25.760
To name the effort, Galton had coined the term eugenics,

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:29.970
a hybrid derived from two Greek words, meaning well-educated.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:39.330
and born. Charles Davenport believed, as Galton did, that selective breeding could

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:46.020
transform the human race. What was needed was a scientific understanding of how

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:51.960
heredity actually worked. And over dinner at Galton's home, Davenport declared his

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:57.760
intention to get to the bottom of it. Davenport said, I'm gonna create a new

00:06:57.760 --> 00:06:59.280
kind of institution.

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:03.880
a station for experimental evolution.

00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:06.700
Not Darwinian natural selection

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:08.040
that you just go out and observe,

00:07:08.750 --> 00:07:12.280
but can we figure out how inheritance works?

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:18.030
Can we do experiments and find the patterns of heredity?

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:26.630
When Davenport sailed for home in December 1902,

00:07:27.410 --> 00:07:29.790
he carried with him not only a letter of recommendation,

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:36.220
signed by Galton but also he later wrote a renewed courage for the study of

00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:46.400
evolution. Davenport and Galton really did imagine that the idea of improving

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:54.540
human heredity was of almost religious significance, of profound moral importance.

00:07:56.620 --> 00:07:59.980
They also believed they were qualified to breed a baby.

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:14.430
race because they believed that they were the best and the brightest. Scarcely more than a year

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:20.920
later, with funding from the Carnegie Institution, Davenport opened his research station on the north

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:29.520
shore of Long Island at Cold Spring Harbor. Situated on 10 acres along Oyster Bay, the place had been

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:29.880
purpose.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:36.420
built for the breeding and analyzing of plants and animals, complete with sprawling garden plots,

00:08:37.180 --> 00:08:46.640
an aviary, and a half dozen tidy enclosures housing chickens, goats, and sheep. By mating

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:53.540
organisms with unusual characteristics, a tailless Manx cat, or a rooster with a black comb,

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:59.900
and then studying their offspring generation after generation, Davenport Hope

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:01.940
to unlock the mystery of evolution.

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:07.380
Davenport wasn't yet thinking much about humans.

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:12.060
He was just absorbing all these different theories

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:14.080
of heredity and trying to figure out

00:09:14.140 --> 00:09:16.720
which ones applied when and under what conditions.

00:09:19.340 --> 00:09:20.760
After scores of experiments,

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:23.480
one theory seemed to stand out from the rest.

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:27.240
The recently discovered work of an Austrian monk

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:29.920
named Gregor Mendel, who'd spent a decade

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:33.240
in the mid-19th century, experimenting with peas.

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:37.320
Mendel learned that there was a pattern

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:40.340
to how pea plants passed down certain traits,

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:43.800
and you could come up with certain ratios

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:46.820
to predict how likely it was

00:09:46.820 --> 00:09:48.940
that a pea plant would look one way or another.

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:52.990
Davenport took that and ran with it.

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:56.200
He goes about breeding all kinds of animals

00:09:56.200 --> 00:09:58.020
looking for the Mendelian ratio.

00:09:59.000 --> 00:09:59.980
And in trait...

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.400
After trait, he seems to find the Mendelian ratio.

00:10:06.710 --> 00:10:11.330
Suddenly, they're beginning to see a mathematical scientific explanation for things that had

00:10:11.330 --> 00:10:13.300
been merely conjectural before.

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:17.180
It's becoming obvious that, in fact, there are these things called genes.

00:10:18.690 --> 00:10:23.500
These units are being transmitted from parents to their offspring, and they're giving rise

00:10:23.500 --> 00:10:24.790
to physical traits.

00:10:36.970 --> 00:10:41.560
By 1906, the work at Cold Spring Harbor had caught the attention of the press

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:47.640
and established Charles Davenport as a rising star in the new science of genetics.

00:10:49.690 --> 00:10:53.210
Thanks to Mendel's laws of heredity, Davenport told one reporter,

00:10:54.050 --> 00:10:57.930
agricultural breeders could now precisely select for desirable traits

00:10:57.930 --> 00:10:59.970
to develop a strain of...

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:03.300
protein-rich wheat, or chicken that laid more eggs.

00:11:03.810 --> 00:11:07.410
The same methods would one day lead, he predicted,

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:10.620
to a rapid and thoroughgoing improvement

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:11.980
of the human race.

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:17.970
Davenport was tantalized by the possibility

00:11:17.970 --> 00:11:21.300
that you could take charge of human evolution.

00:11:22.130 --> 00:11:24.190
And then along comes Mendelian genetics,

00:11:24.750 --> 00:11:26.890
which seems to offer a very powerful tool.

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:29.800
So extrapolating from the work that

00:11:30.280 --> 00:11:37.810
the breeders were doing in animals to the breeding of better human beings was a natural step.

00:11:42.050 --> 00:11:48.710
In 1909, Davenport informed his funder, the Carnegie Institution, that he'd shifted his focus

00:11:48.710 --> 00:11:54.020
from the breeding of cats and roosters to an investigation of human traits.

00:11:55.340 --> 00:11:59.970
Having already found the Mendelian ratio in early...

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:06.360
studies of eye color and hair color and convinced he was on the right track, he now began to

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:13.300
collect data on a wide range of other human characteristics. He sent out a family history

00:12:13.300 --> 00:12:20.140
questionnaire to hundreds of individuals and solicited prisons, hospitals, and educational

00:12:20.140 --> 00:12:27.610
institutions for their records. You can't really do breeding experiments with human beings. Aside

00:12:27.610 --> 00:12:29.970
Aside from the ethics, you just can't live long enough to see...

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:36.380
generations and generations. So it was Davenport's genius to realize if he could collect family

00:12:36.380 --> 00:12:43.240
pedigrees, he could trace family inheritances and try to prove that evolution works for human

00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:50.390
beings the way it works for animals. Davenport's plan was to analyze the pedigree charts for

00:12:50.390 --> 00:12:56.680
Mendelian patterns and to identify the desirable traits human beings might encourage through

00:12:56.680 --> 00:12:59.910
careful breeding and the undesirable ones.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:01.560
they could breed out.

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:17.820
Wouldn't it be a better world if we could wipe out poverty?

00:13:19.540 --> 00:13:21.860
Wouldn't it be a better world if we didn't have criminals?

00:13:23.420 --> 00:13:26.200
Wouldn't it be a better world if everyone behaved themselves?

00:13:27.210 --> 00:13:29.970
And if the reason we have poverty...

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:38.340
poverty and crime is something that's determined by our genes, if we can change that and make

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:44.850
it so that the people who have those bad traits don't pass them down, wouldn't that be a better

00:13:44.850 --> 00:13:45.230
world?

