00:21:37.260 --> 00:21:40.550
Henry Goddard said, you know, it takes an expert

00:21:40.550 --> 00:21:43.920
to identify the true menace of feeble-mindedness.

00:21:44.660 --> 00:21:47.020
So someone you're sitting next to at a restaurant

00:21:47.020 --> 00:21:49.800
or in a theater could look perfectly normal to you.

00:21:49.860 --> 00:21:53.900
And it only takes one feeble-minded person

00:21:53.900 --> 00:21:56.560
marrying another one, even someone who's not feeble-minded,

00:21:57.500 --> 00:21:59.980
to create generations of feeble-mindedness.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:07.970
feeble-mindedness. What it did is up the stakes of feeble-mindedness by claiming that it was a

00:22:07.970 --> 00:22:24.100
hidden menace that was more difficult to pinpoint than people might think. By early 1910, Charles

00:22:24.100 --> 00:22:29.510
Davenport was convinced that certain human traits were passed down in a predictable way.

00:22:30.670 --> 00:22:33.950
and that American society could be dramatically improved

00:22:33.950 --> 00:22:36.580
if only reproduction were controlled.

00:22:38.430 --> 00:22:39.890
Anxious to spread the word,

00:22:40.580 --> 00:22:43.380
he began to lay plans for a new institution

00:22:43.660 --> 00:22:46.700
dedicated to eugenic research and education.

00:22:52.490 --> 00:22:54.190
February, in search of a patron,

00:22:55.140 --> 00:22:56.580
Davenport traveled to New York

00:22:57.530 --> 00:22:59.490
to lunch with Mrs. E. H. Harriman.

00:23:00.320 --> 00:23:03.220
widow of a recently deceased railroad magnate.

00:23:05.640 --> 00:23:08.560
Davenport's pitch to Mrs. Harriman was to say,

00:23:08.820 --> 00:23:11.440
right now you give your money to all kinds of good organizations.

00:23:11.440 --> 00:23:13.720
They feed the poor, they clothe the poor,

00:23:13.880 --> 00:23:17.500
they do many wonderful things, but it's never-ending.

00:23:18.650 --> 00:23:22.690
With eugenics, we eventually won't need your philanthropy and charity

00:23:22.690 --> 00:23:26.500
because we'll solve the problems that right now you're just throwing money at.

00:23:27.870 --> 00:23:29.850
He persuaded Mrs. Harriman.

00:23:30.380 --> 00:23:36.220
that the future of the country was at stake and that only a eugenic project could save it.

00:23:38.010 --> 00:23:42.970
Charles Davenport says, all you people who think that if we just educate the poor,

00:23:43.450 --> 00:23:46.790
if we just give them charity, if we just reform their environment,

00:23:46.990 --> 00:23:52.960
even the poor can rise to our level, forget about it. That's just sentimental hogwash.

00:23:53.990 --> 00:23:59.000
It's not the environment that makes you what you are, it's your genetic inheritance from your parents.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:05.600
now, yes, let's regulate the matings of human beings, eliminate the bad genes from the population,

00:24:05.820 --> 00:24:14.530
and keep the fittest genes in the gene pool. By limiting the birth of people who were deemed

00:24:14.530 --> 00:24:22.890
to be unfit, you were by definition enhancing the stock of human society. And so there was

00:24:22.890 --> 00:24:29.900
this social mission of really fighting dependency, fighting crime through eugenics.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:38.680
The idea was that eugenics would solve all of these broader social problems if enacted in a robust way.

00:24:40.760 --> 00:24:44.900
Mrs. Harriman was a great believer in the importance of proper matings.

00:24:45.780 --> 00:24:55.040
She credited her late husband's interest in horse breeding for that, and she enthusiastically pledged to finance Davenport's eugenic enterprise.

00:24:57.420 --> 00:24:59.680
It was, Davenport later wrote in his journal,

00:25:00.510 --> 00:25:02.710
a red-letter day for humanity.

00:25:09.160 --> 00:25:14.770
The impulse to perfect humanity is an ancient aspiration.

00:25:16.180 --> 00:25:20.010
The idea that somehow or the other that you can get the best humans

00:25:20.010 --> 00:25:27.440
by selectively breeding the best, most fit, hardiest, most beautiful,

00:25:28.160 --> 00:25:29.240
it's an ancient desire.

00:25:30.380 --> 00:25:32.540
You find it in Sanskrit texts.

00:25:32.680 --> 00:25:34.180
You find it in Greek texts.

00:25:35.350 --> 00:25:38.530
The trouble is that only some human beings can dictate

00:25:38.530 --> 00:25:43.230
or decide what the correct features might be.

00:25:44.190 --> 00:25:44.970
Who decides?

00:25:49.060 --> 00:25:50.920
Charles Davenport thought,

00:25:51.400 --> 00:25:53.520
by breeding a superior race of people,

00:25:53.920 --> 00:25:57.820
we can bring about the millennial kingdom on earth.

00:26:00.060 --> 00:26:05.760
The problem with utopias is that they set a set of aspirations

00:26:05.760 --> 00:26:10.260
that then blind you to a certain set of consequences,

00:26:10.260 --> 00:26:12.820
and that can be dangerous.

00:26:20.520 --> 00:26:26.360
In October 1910, on an 80-acre plot adjacent to the Cold Spring Harbor campus,

00:26:27.410 --> 00:26:29.970
Charles Davenport opened the doors of his new...

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:37.730
Institute. It was a modest structure built for a grand purpose, to house hereditary information

00:26:37.730 --> 00:26:44.970
on American families and use it to guide the reproductive choices of the nation. He called it

00:26:44.970 --> 00:26:54.640
the Eugenics Record Office. Eugenic ideas were very much floating around as early as 1880,

00:26:55.520 --> 00:26:59.960
but Davenport gave eugenics teeth. He was institutionalized.

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:07.180
traditionalizing eugenics. He was marshalling people around a research program. Davenport

00:27:07.180 --> 00:27:12.560
already had assembled a prestigious board of scientific directors, among them prominent

00:27:12.560 --> 00:27:20.800
scientists, physicians, and famed inventor Alexander Graham Bell. Day-to-day operations,

00:27:20.960 --> 00:27:26.440
meanwhile, would be overseen by Harry Laughlin, a high school superintendent from the Midwest

00:27:26.440 --> 00:27:29.860
with a lifelong passion for poultry breeding.

00:27:31.980 --> 00:27:36.260
Laughlin was a very zealous proponent of eugenics,

00:27:36.300 --> 00:27:38.200
and in that sense, he got along well with Davenport.

00:27:38.670 --> 00:27:40.950
They both believed in the mission, they believed in the cause.

00:27:42.040 --> 00:27:45.360
For Davenport, a lot of it was about what you could do in the laboratory

00:27:45.360 --> 00:27:47.800
and how you can analyze the data.

00:27:48.290 --> 00:27:49.790
For Laughlin, a lot of it was about,

00:27:49.930 --> 00:27:51.950
well, how are we going to get out there in the world

00:27:51.950 --> 00:27:53.960
and change the direction of history?

00:27:56.940 --> 00:27:59.980
To gather new disciples to the cause and to...

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:05.840
aid in the collection of data, Laughlin and Davenport launched an academic program, which

00:28:05.840 --> 00:28:14.240
offered training in eugenic field research techniques. Over the course of six weeks each

00:28:14.240 --> 00:28:21.600
summer, recent college graduates from Vassar, Harvard, Oberlin were taught how to investigate

00:28:21.600 --> 00:28:29.020
family histories, how to conduct interviews, and make eugenically useful measurements, and how to

00:28:29.020 --> 00:28:29.820
chart family.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:37.650
pedigrees and analyze them. Then, at a salary of $75 a month, came a year's work in the field.

00:28:39.370 --> 00:28:45.720
Armed with the official trait book, which assigned numerical codes to a broad spectrum of human

00:28:45.720 --> 00:28:53.580
characteristics, the newly minted researchers fanned out to study delinquents in the Juvenile

00:28:53.580 --> 00:28:59.780
Psychopathic Institute of Chicago, the insane, at the New Jersey State Hospital at Matawan.

00:29:01.020 --> 00:29:11.200
albinos in massachusetts circus families at coney island the amish in pennsylvania they would go

00:29:11.200 --> 00:29:18.140
into some holler in virginia and find some family that seemed to have a lot of alcoholics and

00:29:18.290 --> 00:29:24.280
criminals and you know other ne'er-do-wells and they would go aha we've found a family

00:29:24.280 --> 00:29:29.970
with terrible genes and they'd interview someone and they'd say oh yeah you know johnson

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:32.300
seems a little slow, but I knew his uncle,

00:29:32.500 --> 00:29:33.740
and his uncle was a big drunk,

00:29:33.880 --> 00:29:35.620
and they'd write that down, uncle, a big drunk.

00:29:36.020 --> 00:29:38.550
So they would come back with all this evidence

00:29:38.550 --> 00:29:41.270
of the way in which human traits were inherited.

00:29:41.470 --> 00:29:47.170
Of course, the reliability of the data was not questioned,

00:29:47.860 --> 00:29:50.520
even though it was based on interpretation,

00:29:50.880 --> 00:29:54.020
impression, recollection by the living members

00:29:54.020 --> 00:29:55.360
of the family with whom they spoke.

00:29:56.120 --> 00:29:56.680
No checking.

00:30:00.670 --> 00:30:06.620
Year by year, as trainees rotated out of the summer program and into positions at universities,

00:30:07.000 --> 00:30:13.040
hospitals, and mental institutions, Davenport's assumptions and methods of fieldwork gained

00:30:13.040 --> 00:30:22.200
currency all across the country. And year by year, the data accumulated. Stored in fireproof cabinets

00:30:22.200 --> 00:30:28.420
and intricately indexed, it comprised, as Scientific American noted, a sort of inventory

00:30:28.420 --> 00:30:29.680
of the blood of the community.

00:30:31.440 --> 00:30:36.630
and supplied grist for a multitude of books, pamphlets, lectures, and press releases

00:30:36.780 --> 00:30:41.450
regarding the danger of so-called inferior germplasm.

00:30:45.390 --> 00:30:48.990
They're using the family records that are stored in the fireproof vault

00:30:49.660 --> 00:30:57.460
to prove all these traits, not just physical, but mental traits, moral traits, are caused by genes.

00:30:58.250 --> 00:30:59.610
There's nothing you can do about it.

00:31:00.380 --> 00:31:03.570
This is cold, hard, pure science.

00:31:05.180 --> 00:31:10.810
Just as we have strains of scholars, of military men, Davenport told the New York Times,

00:31:11.690 --> 00:31:18.560
we have strains of paupers, of sex offenders, strains with strong tendencies toward larceny,

00:31:18.820 --> 00:31:21.520
assault, lying, running away.

00:31:22.770 --> 00:31:25.980
The cost to society of these strains is enormous.

00:31:27.500 --> 00:31:29.500
Davenport took this basic idea.

00:31:30.410 --> 00:31:38.840
applied it far more widely than it had ever been before and really promoted this probabilistic idea

00:31:38.840 --> 00:31:46.040
as if it were a deterministic one that is to say it's not just likelihoods but in some ways

00:31:46.040 --> 00:31:56.480
we're dealing with certainties and that idea really sold by the time the eugenics record

00:31:56.480 --> 00:31:59.600
office issued its first official report in 1913.

00:32:00.600 --> 00:32:03.800
many Americans had begun to see the wisdom in eugenics.

00:32:05.500 --> 00:32:11.390
I agree with you that society has no business to permit degenerates to reproduce their kind.

00:32:11.940 --> 00:32:15.140
Former President Theodore Roosevelt wrote Davenport that year.

00:32:16.330 --> 00:32:20.250
It is really extraordinary that our people refuse to apply to human beings

00:32:20.600 --> 00:32:26.690
such knowledge as every successful farmer is obliged to apply to his own stock breeding.

00:32:30.030 --> 00:32:34.810
if you're going to be in the business of breeding you're going to have to convince

00:32:34.810 --> 00:32:41.360
thought leaders and politicians, most especially the government, to begin a kind of unprecedented

00:32:41.360 --> 00:32:48.050
program. So they understood from the beginning that they needed to persuade those who were in

00:32:48.050 --> 00:32:55.820
a position to do something about it that it was possible, indeed desirable. The eugenicists

00:32:55.820 --> 00:32:59.680
thought that people's base interests are just self-serving, selfish.

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:04.940
And if you just leave them to themselves, they're going to evolve in all these random, dumb directions.

00:33:06.750 --> 00:33:13.640
The Eugenics Record Office recommended both widespread eugenic education and aggressive government intervention.

00:33:14.880 --> 00:33:27.030
Laws that would keep defectives out of the country, prohibit them from marrying, and prevent them from becoming parents by segregating them in asylums throughout their reproductive years.

00:33:30.670 --> 00:33:36.440
Also recommended was a new and somewhat controversial surgical procedure known as sterilization.

00:33:38.290 --> 00:33:44.890
By cutting and sealing organs involved in reproduction, both men and women could be made infertile.

00:33:46.660 --> 00:33:51.460
So far, the technique had been used primarily on criminals, particularly sex offenders,

00:33:52.040 --> 00:33:54.220
and it was thought to have a curative effect.

00:33:56.080 --> 00:33:59.980
Harry Laughlin envisioned a broader application as a...

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:04.730
a eugenic tool that would eliminate defective germplasm once and for all.

00:34:05.860 --> 00:34:09.480
Harry Laughlin really has this political vision of what we can do with eugenics,

00:34:09.690 --> 00:34:14.860
and he said in order for eugenic sterilization to really do what had to be done,

00:34:15.060 --> 00:34:17.660
15 million Americans would have to go under the knife.

00:34:21.720 --> 00:34:25.980
The idea was that eugenics was for the common good,

00:34:26.000 --> 00:34:29.680
and by implementing the science of heredity.

00:34:30.160 --> 00:34:33.160
They could protect America and strengthen America.

00:34:34.940 --> 00:34:37.540
They thought of it as the beginning of a revolution,

00:34:38.440 --> 00:34:39.500
of a religious movement.

00:34:40.870 --> 00:34:42.730
You have to start with a few converts,

00:34:42.790 --> 00:34:46.320
and then you try to grow it into a bigger movement.

00:34:47.300 --> 00:34:50.280
All that seemed exciting and full of possibility.

00:34:50.300 --> 00:34:53.730
They were going to create a new world.

00:35:08.990 --> 00:35:14.980
The eugenics movement that Charles Davenport had launched rested on Mendel's Laws of Inheritance,

00:35:14.980 --> 00:35:21.640
which assumed each trait was governed by one gene and was passed down in predictable patterns.

00:35:21.640 --> 00:35:28.520
But for all of Davenport's certainty about the gene, there remained open questions.

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:36.490
the gene's physical properties and its location within the cell and the means by which it

00:35:36.490 --> 00:35:42.990
accomplished its function. All over the world, scientists looked to fast-breeding organisms

00:35:42.990 --> 00:35:49.480
in search of clues. Some focused their experiments on the sea urchin, which turned out a new

00:35:49.480 --> 00:35:57.650
generation each year. Others on the even speedier mealworm, with its larva-to-larva cycle of four

00:35:57.650 --> 00:35:58.070
months.

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:07.240
Thomas Hunt Morgan, the organism of choice was the fruit fly, which was capable of reproducing

00:36:07.520 --> 00:36:14.600
in just 10 days. The organism breeds so quickly that Morgan is able to see things that the

00:36:14.600 --> 00:36:24.160
genesis cannot because he's watching mutations move across multiple generations. By 1913,

00:36:24.420 --> 00:36:29.560
Morgan had been studying fruit flies for so long that his laboratory at Columbia University

00:36:30.250 --> 00:36:38.990
was known simply as the Fly Room and his assistants the Fly Boys. For nearly a decade they'd been

00:36:38.990 --> 00:36:45.580
holed up there on the sixth floor of Skimmerhorn Hall, breeding flies in half-pint milk bottles

00:36:45.730 --> 00:36:53.060
pilfered from the campus cafeteria. Thousands upon thousands of mutants were crossed and the

00:36:53.060 --> 00:36:59.970
results meticulously recorded. White-eyed, bristled, red-eyed, short-eyed,

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:06.600
When the data was collated, Morgan made a startling discovery.

00:37:07.500 --> 00:37:12.760
The mechanism of heredity in flies was far more complex than in Mendel's peas.

00:37:14.190 --> 00:37:19.000
Gregor Mendel thought that every gene was its own unique discrete entity.

00:37:19.950 --> 00:37:22.490
Morgan showed that, in fact, that's not the case.

00:37:22.630 --> 00:37:25.590
That, in fact, genes live, genes have a physical entity.

00:37:25.750 --> 00:37:27.290
They live in chromosomes.

00:37:27.800 --> 00:37:29.980
Because they live in chromosomes, often they travel and...

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:40.250
Pax. Morgan's work complicates the idea of simple eugenics because you don't just pick one thing

00:37:40.250 --> 00:37:46.760
out of one drawer, a second thing out of another drawer, until you get your ideal child. It's not

00:37:46.760 --> 00:37:56.050
so easy to pick and choose what your next generation might be. It makes sense in pea plants,

00:37:56.050 --> 00:37:59.380
It makes sense in cattle, it should make sense in humans.

00:38:00.760 --> 00:38:07.080
But there were no experiments that really could support Davenport's theory.

00:38:09.950 --> 00:38:14.620
Thomas Hunt Morgan was a believer in the transformative power of eugenics.

00:38:15.500 --> 00:38:18.960
He had served on the board at the Eugenics Record Office since it opened.

00:38:20.150 --> 00:38:22.670
But based on the lessons he'd learned in the fly room,

00:38:23.620 --> 00:38:29.590
it seemed clear that eugenic science, such as it was, had no business informing American laws.

00:38:32.720 --> 00:38:37.460
If the eugenicists want to do this sort of thing, well and good, Morgan wrote a friend,

00:38:38.440 --> 00:38:41.800
but I think it is just as well for some of us to set a better standard

00:38:41.800 --> 00:38:45.220
and not appear as participators in the show.

00:38:47.910 --> 00:38:52.780
Morgan writes a letter saying, I'm going to ask to be taken off this letterhead.

00:38:54.050 --> 00:38:58.100
I study fruit flies and I can't figure out how their eyes work.

00:38:58.830 --> 00:38:59.970
I can't figure out which...

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:02.790
one's going to inherit certain kinds of wings.

00:39:03.590 --> 00:39:07.760
And you seem to be saying you can understand who's

00:39:07.760 --> 00:39:10.340
going to inherit something as vague as criminality

00:39:10.340 --> 00:39:11.960
or pauperism.

00:39:12.710 --> 00:39:14.900
So he backed away, but privately.

00:39:16.600 --> 00:39:19.840
Morgan's withdrawal from the record office was regrettable.

00:39:20.500 --> 00:39:22.360
But Davenport was undeterred.

00:39:23.950 --> 00:39:25.710
At this point, the eugenics movement

00:39:25.710 --> 00:39:28.880
would not be stalled by the minutia of science.

00:39:31.600 --> 00:39:35.700
What genes are is a great biological and biochemical question.

00:39:36.370 --> 00:39:41.560
But there's a kind of Yankee practicality about eugenics.

00:39:41.700 --> 00:39:42.980
Let's get this job done.

00:39:44.100 --> 00:39:45.880
And so they move right along.

00:39:54.220 --> 00:39:59.970
When the Panama Pacific International Exposition opened in San Francisco on the morning of February...

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:08.820
20th, 1915, 100,000 people streamed through its turnstiles. Over the next nine months,

00:40:09.320 --> 00:40:16.990
the number would reach more than 18 million. Billed as an encyclopedia of modern achievement,

00:40:18.040 --> 00:40:25.460
the fair offered a dizzying array of diversions and curiosities. A 23-minute ride over a functioning

00:40:25.460 --> 00:40:28.020
replica of the recently completed Panama Canal.

00:40:30.280 --> 00:40:33.760
An assembly line that turned out 18 Model Ts a day.

00:40:34.400 --> 00:40:40.160
A 57-tier tower built entirely of Heinz condiment products.

00:40:41.000 --> 00:40:48.630
The Panama Pacific Expo was a celebration of science, efficiency, engineering.

00:40:49.400 --> 00:40:54.770
It was an opportunity for the United States to demonstrate the power of science and technology

00:40:55.240 --> 00:40:59.980
and also a utopian vision looking towards the future.

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:06.240
Nowhere did the future look brighter than from the Race Betterment exhibit.

00:41:07.920 --> 00:41:15.140
Housed in the Palace of Education, the display featured imposing plaster casts of Atlas, Venus, and Apollo,

00:41:16.750 --> 00:41:21.010
a collection of medical instruments used to gauge human biological capacity,

00:41:22.240 --> 00:41:29.110
and a welter of charts, graphs, and lists that outline the way eugenics would better the human race.

00:41:32.080 --> 00:41:35.220
All of it was the work of Dr. John Harvey Kellogg,

00:41:36.000 --> 00:41:39.740
a fierce proponent of what he called biologic living.

00:41:43.340 --> 00:41:47.060
John Harvey Kellogg was an incredibly energetic man.

00:41:47.840 --> 00:41:53.180
He was a health reformer and a physician and an amazing entrepreneur.

00:41:53.180 --> 00:41:59.690
And he developed all these regimens and invented different medical instruments.

00:42:00.660 --> 00:42:02.700
had a whole dietary plan.

00:42:03.460 --> 00:42:06.640
He was obsessed with cleanliness, with purity,

00:42:06.640 --> 00:42:10.880
and he believed that the key to reforming society

00:42:10.940 --> 00:42:14.460
is to cleanse our bowels on a regular basis.

00:42:14.460 --> 00:42:18.560
He invents something called cornflakes

00:42:18.560 --> 00:42:21.060
to help cleanse your bowels,

00:42:21.840 --> 00:42:26.310
and he had a spa in Battle Creek, Michigan.

00:42:27.300 --> 00:42:29.960
Lots of eugenicists came to the Battle Creek.

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:36.410
sanatorium to have their bowels cleansed and to talk about eugenics.

00:42:37.600 --> 00:42:44.400
For Kellogg, eugenics made perfect sense. It was about health. He linked these views

00:42:44.400 --> 00:42:49.230
about heredity, which were difficult to change, with these ideas about what human beings can do

00:42:49.230 --> 00:42:56.020
to improve themselves. John Harvey Kellogg believed that the environment can affect the gene,

00:42:56.230 --> 00:42:59.190
that consuming alcohol or consuming meat.

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:04.500
could lead to genetic inferiority in offspring.

00:43:04.940 --> 00:43:08.880
So there's more than one way to improve heredity.

00:43:14.130 --> 00:43:18.210
At the expo, Kellogg sought to usher his brand of eugenics

00:43:18.210 --> 00:43:19.690
onto the national stage.

00:43:21.440 --> 00:43:23.460
With assistance from Charles Davenport,

00:43:23.920 --> 00:43:26.440
who had supplied him with both data and contacts,

00:43:27.360 --> 00:43:29.980
Kellogg had organized not only the Race Betterment Exhibit,

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:33.270
but also a major eugenics conference at the fair.

00:43:37.550 --> 00:43:39.510
The turnout exceeded expectation,

00:43:40.350 --> 00:43:42.690
drawing reform-minded medical professionals,

00:43:43.370 --> 00:43:46.520
university presidents, conservationists,

00:43:46.800 --> 00:43:49.920
and business leaders from all over the country

00:43:49.920 --> 00:43:51.840
and across the political spectrum.

00:43:53.820 --> 00:43:56.160
Eugenics had a little bit of something for everyone.

00:43:56.800 --> 00:43:58.540
So if you're a social hygienist,

00:43:58.860 --> 00:43:59.980
you're interested in what you're doing.

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:03.560
