01:03:42.210 --> 01:03:43.810
The congressional record is filled

01:03:43.810 --> 01:03:45.960
with congressmen reading excerpts

01:03:45.960 --> 01:03:47.520
from The Passing of the Great Race,

01:03:48.190 --> 01:03:50.260
Madison Grant's book, on the floor of Congress.

01:03:51.080 --> 01:03:53.220
And so the restrictionists win the day,

01:03:53.280 --> 01:03:56.080
and Congress passes immigration restriction legislation.

01:03:58.160 --> 01:03:59.960
On May 26, 19...

01:04:00.000 --> 01:04:04.820
In 1924, President Calvin Coolidge signed the Restriction Act into law.

01:04:06.980 --> 01:04:12.670
Madison Grant hailed it as one of the greatest steps forward in the history of this country.

01:04:14.840 --> 01:04:20.440
They shut the door and reduced immigration to the United States by 97 percent.

01:04:20.960 --> 01:04:24.000
The door was shut and it didn't open again for 40 years.

01:04:25.050 --> 01:04:29.520
And in a very real sense, this was a political policy victory.

01:04:30.000 --> 01:04:37.980
for eugenics the new policy would help the nation to remain as one congressman said on the house

01:04:37.980 --> 01:04:46.400
floor the home of a great people english-speaking a white race with great ideals the christian

01:04:46.400 --> 01:04:57.100
religion one race one country one destiny it was really a reversal of you know give us

01:04:57.100 --> 01:04:59.980
you're tired and you're huddled masses and it's

01:05:00.000 --> 01:05:05.210
It sends a message that the open arms of Ellis Island are now closed.

01:05:08.280 --> 01:05:13.710
For many of those across the Atlantic who would pin their hopes on America in the years to come,

01:05:14.690 --> 01:05:16.790
the consequences would be dire.

01:05:18.230 --> 01:05:23.750
Congress passed this law and closed the door on Jews in Eastern Europe and Germany

01:05:23.750 --> 01:05:26.010
who were trying to flee the Nazis.

01:05:27.090 --> 01:05:29.490
Otto Frank wrote to the U.S. State Department,

01:05:30.000 --> 01:05:34.640
to get visas for his family and he wrote repeatedly and he had connections and he was

01:05:34.640 --> 01:05:42.710
turned down because of this law. We think about Anne Frank dying in a concentration camp because

01:05:42.710 --> 01:05:48.240
the Germans thought the Jews were genetically inferior, but to some extent Anne Frank died

01:05:48.240 --> 01:05:52.020
in a concentration camp because the U.S. Congress believed that as well.

01:06:00.090 --> 01:06:12.400
people who have transmissible diseases should not have children. No couple who has the disease of

01:06:12.400 --> 01:06:21.170
feeble-mindedness or insanity or epilepsy should have children. Babies should not be brought into

01:06:21.170 --> 01:06:29.300
the well when the father's income is obviously inadequate to provide for his food.

01:06:30.350 --> 01:06:31.710
Clothing or shelter.

01:06:35.300 --> 01:06:39.850
On August 5th, 1926, a crowd gathered at Vassar College

01:06:39.850 --> 01:06:42.840
to hear a lecture given by Margaret Sanger,

01:06:43.580 --> 01:06:46.620
the controversial founder of the American Birth Control League.

01:06:48.720 --> 01:06:50.720
Sanger's reputation preceded her.

01:06:51.660 --> 01:06:55.380
In her dozen years as a crusader for contraception and family planning,

01:06:56.240 --> 01:06:59.590
she'd been denounced, jeered, and jailed repeatedly.

01:07:01.150 --> 01:07:07.760
Now she'd undertaken a cross-country speaking tour intended to bolster her cause by linking it to eugenics.

01:07:09.480 --> 01:07:17.900
Margaret Sanger was laser beam focused on promoting birth control, which she saw as a liberatory agent for women.

01:07:19.600 --> 01:07:23.970
It was a hard push. Reproductive rights, contraception.

01:07:24.940 --> 01:07:29.660
Her embrace of the eugenicists was a way of getting some influential.

01:07:30.060 --> 01:07:38.250
powerful allies behind her cause. The question of race betterment, Sanger told the Vassar audience,

01:07:38.880 --> 01:07:44.430
is one of immediate concern. And I am glad to say that the government has already taken certain

01:07:44.430 --> 01:07:51.830
steps to control the quality of our population through the drastic immigration laws. But while

01:07:51.830 --> 01:07:57.890
we close our gates to the so-called undesirables from other countries, we make no attempt to

01:07:57.890 --> 01:07:59.970
discourage or cut down the rest of the world.

01:08:00.000 --> 01:08:04.120
rapid multiplication of the unfit and undesirable at home.

01:08:07.020 --> 01:08:13.050
Margaret Sanger is struggling to open a conversation at a time when public discussion of birth

01:08:13.050 --> 01:08:20.310
control, let alone access to birth control, was illegal. But her views are fairly persistent

01:08:20.310 --> 01:08:26.220
with regard to issues of biological inferiority. You could argue that they're strategic,

01:08:26.970 --> 01:08:29.790
but the difference is not that significant.

01:08:30.000 --> 01:08:32.340
from the standpoint of those listening to her words.

01:08:33.710 --> 01:08:38.330
Once birth control is packaged as a way of improving the human race,

01:08:38.330 --> 01:08:40.720
it seems more manageable.

01:08:41.810 --> 01:08:45.680
There are a lot of people that were on the fence that she convinced

01:08:45.680 --> 01:08:50.470
to embrace birth control because of its eugenic potential.

01:08:52.150 --> 01:08:56.110
It was being labeled a birth control activist that was truly controversial.

01:08:56.540 --> 01:08:59.420
Being a eugenicist was far more acceptable.

01:09:05.020 --> 01:09:13.370
Amid the many American enthusiasms of the 1920s, skimpy dresses, dance marathons, mahjong,

01:09:14.720 --> 01:09:18.270
breeding a better human race was perhaps the most unlikely.

01:09:19.670 --> 01:09:23.360
But by the middle years of the decade, the notion was everywhere.

01:09:24.780 --> 01:09:29.660
Included in the curriculum met more than 350 American colleges and universities.

01:09:30.540 --> 01:09:38.530
among them harvard northwestern and the university of california at berkeley eugenics also was

01:09:38.530 --> 01:09:46.210
preached from pulpits promoted on lecture circuits and appropriated to sell everything from newfangled

01:09:46.210 --> 01:09:55.620
beauty treatments to children's toys disseminated by a host of popularizers and at times diluted

01:09:55.620 --> 01:09:57.260
distorted, or both.

01:09:58.460 --> 01:09:59.960
The eugenic creed filled

01:10:00.000 --> 01:10:06.780
down to the masses through magazine articles, advice manuals, even a movie

01:10:07.100 --> 01:10:14.830
called Are You Fit to Marry? This was really something that permeated the

01:10:14.830 --> 01:10:19.200
culture. It was really a craze. It was something people were excited about.

01:10:21.260 --> 01:10:26.060
Eugenics starts to trickle into mainstream popular culture in the 1920s

01:10:26.560 --> 01:10:29.970
and it says to individual Americans if you

01:10:30.000 --> 01:10:34.520
If you want your society to improve, you have to marry the right person, you have to have

01:10:34.520 --> 01:10:40.020
healthy children, you have obligations to the human race and to your country.

01:10:41.480 --> 01:10:47.470
As one newlywed confessed in a letter to his local eugenics society, my wife and I are

01:10:47.470 --> 01:10:53.660
both extremely tall and this worries us as we do not wish to bring abnormally tall children

01:10:53.660 --> 01:10:54.520
into the world.

01:11:01.980 --> 01:11:09.000
At state and county fairs across the country, in Massachusetts, Kansas, Georgia, Texas,

01:11:10.360 --> 01:11:17.090
a human stock contest known as Fitter Families for Future Firesides drew throngs.

01:11:18.940 --> 01:11:25.570
Sponsored by the American Eugenics Society, a propaganda organization run by the movement's evangelists,

01:11:25.570 --> 01:11:29.970
Harry Laughlin and Madison Grant, the competition offered a prim...

01:11:30.000 --> 01:11:34.020
on eugenics, disguised as wholesome family entertainment.

01:11:37.640 --> 01:11:41.600
What the American Eugenics Society realized is that if you're going to spread a message

01:11:41.600 --> 01:11:46.430
about eugenics, you have to get people involved in more than just reading something in a popular

01:11:46.430 --> 01:11:46.950
magazine.

01:11:48.460 --> 01:11:51.400
Eugenics is an all-encompassing creed.

01:11:51.560 --> 01:11:52.300
It's a faith.

01:11:52.480 --> 01:11:53.340
It's a religion.

01:11:54.200 --> 01:11:59.910
Harry Laughlin and Madison Grant understood we need the people to be converted to this religion.

01:12:00.000 --> 01:12:04.560
so that everyone will understand, if I am eugenically superior,

01:12:04.580 --> 01:12:09.280
I cannot date and certainly cannot mate with a eugenically unfit person.

01:12:11.470 --> 01:12:14.390
Fitter Families contestants came from miles around,

01:12:15.320 --> 01:12:17.060
often dressed in their Sunday best,

01:12:17.080 --> 01:12:21.270
and submitted themselves to a rigorous three-hour inspection.

01:12:23.300 --> 01:12:25.640
Straight, healthy teeth earned them high marks,

01:12:26.380 --> 01:12:29.970
as did musical talent or a family history of long...

01:12:30.000 --> 01:12:37.790
longevity, disease, or disability, even a lame grandmother or an epileptic uncle was

01:12:37.790 --> 01:12:38.450
a demerit.

01:12:39.980 --> 01:12:45.080
While the stock judges are testing the Holsteins, jerseys, and whitefaces in the stock pavilion,

01:12:45.710 --> 01:12:51.700
one contest organizer said, we are judging the Joneses, Smiths, and the Johnsons.

01:12:54.100 --> 01:12:59.470
Just as they would have a contest who had bred the best cows, who had bred the best sheep.

01:13:00.190 --> 01:13:01.950
who had bred the best children.

01:13:02.760 --> 01:13:04.020
And at the end of the state fair,

01:13:04.340 --> 01:13:07.340
the eugenic-winning family, the Fitter family,

01:13:07.740 --> 01:13:09.240
would be driven down the midway

01:13:09.240 --> 01:13:13.080
and wave to the people and show off their ribbons.

01:13:15.360 --> 01:13:19.020
By the 1920s, eugenics was a household word.

01:13:19.500 --> 01:13:22.660
A generation of people grows up thinking of this word

01:13:22.910 --> 01:13:26.400
as an aspiration, healthy babies,

01:13:27.020 --> 01:13:28.480
and as a warning.

01:13:30.000 --> 01:13:36.170
read it in school. They've heard it at church. It has become part of the consciousness of the

01:13:36.170 --> 01:13:46.880
country. So pervasive was the impulse to human improvement, even prominent African-Americans

01:13:46.880 --> 01:13:54.150
took up the theme. W.E.B. Du Bois, one of the founders of the National Association for the

01:13:54.150 --> 01:13:59.770
Advancement of Colored People, maintained that the best of the black race, what he called

01:14:00.090 --> 01:14:03.240
The talented 10th was the hope for the future.

01:14:05.350 --> 01:14:12.380
The Negro, Du Bois declared, must begin to breed for brains, for efficiency, for beauty.

01:14:13.950 --> 01:14:22.020
Du Bois' ideas are fundamentally about combating prejudice, but at the same time, he talked

01:14:22.070 --> 01:14:29.760
about and embraced the notion that not all blacks were equally gifted and equally talented.

01:14:30.000 --> 01:14:38.860
and that the future of African Americans should hinge on the future procreation of the talented.

01:14:40.080 --> 01:14:45.950
Those ideas really are resonant with eugenic ideals of the time.

01:14:48.640 --> 01:14:54.300
Eugenics became a really powerful ideology because it made sense to a lot of different groups

01:14:54.300 --> 01:14:57.120
who were concerned about disparate things.

01:14:57.970 --> 01:14:59.970
Part of the draw is how...

01:15:00.000 --> 01:15:03.340
Science can make us better human beings,

01:15:03.440 --> 01:15:04.880
that we can engineer ourselves

01:15:04.880 --> 01:15:06.680
into being even better than we are,

01:15:07.180 --> 01:15:10.140
and viewing that as a source of progress.

01:15:14.720 --> 01:15:17.160
The eugenics movement of the early 20th century

01:15:17.160 --> 01:15:20.900
got traction because the slogans were simple,

01:15:21.470 --> 01:15:23.970
things like better babies and happy families.

01:15:25.270 --> 01:15:27.620
On the face of it, you know, better babies,

01:15:27.940 --> 01:15:29.760
healthier babies, what's not to like?

01:15:31.050 --> 01:15:39.240
It would have taken considerable effort to demonstrate to people what that simple slogan was actually hiding.

01:15:46.500 --> 01:15:52.260
In September 1924, at the Virginia Colony for the Epileptic and Feeble-Minded,

01:15:52.860 --> 01:15:59.980
the colony's board of directors met to discuss the case of patient 1692, a 17-year-old.

01:16:00.000 --> 01:16:07.480
year old named Carrie Buck. She'd been admitted to the colony several months before at the request

01:16:07.480 --> 01:16:16.380
of her foster parents, who claimed that they could no longer control or care for her. Carrie Buck

01:16:16.380 --> 01:16:23.760
had been raised by a foster family, not a nice family. She is rented out to other people in the

01:16:23.760 --> 01:16:29.520
community to do house cleaning, and she's pulled out of school after fifth grade, even though she's

01:16:29.520 --> 01:16:29.980
doing very well.

01:16:30.000 --> 01:16:38.040
well and is a perfectly good student. Then a nephew of her foster mother rapes her and she gets

01:16:38.040 --> 01:16:45.090
pregnant and they want to get rid of her. By the time her daughter was born, the state had labeled

01:16:45.090 --> 01:16:52.240
Buck morally delinquent for having given birth out of wedlock, diagnosed her a middle grade moron,

01:16:52.930 --> 01:16:59.120
and confined her to the colony. Sexual delinquent, sexually immoral.

01:17:00.000 --> 01:17:01.720
These terms are intentionally vague.

01:17:02.580 --> 01:17:06.910
A moral tendency could be that a woman had been sexually abused,

01:17:07.210 --> 01:17:09.510
it could mean she was going out late at night,

01:17:09.980 --> 01:17:11.300
it could mean she's a prostitute.

01:17:12.650 --> 01:17:17.930
If you're morally deficient, that's evidence that you're mentally deficient, and vice versa.

01:17:18.840 --> 01:17:21.040
So, the state needs to intervene.

01:17:23.390 --> 01:17:26.270
The question before the colony's board of directors now

01:17:26.870 --> 01:17:29.830
was whether or not to sterilize Carrie Buck.

01:17:31.500 --> 01:17:34.120
She lands at the Colony for Epileptics and Feeble-Minded

01:17:34.240 --> 01:17:39.140
right when Virginia has passed a eugenic sterilization law,

01:17:39.730 --> 01:17:42.190
and the lawyer for the state hospitals

01:17:42.190 --> 01:17:46.110
really wants there to be a test before sterilizations occur.

01:17:47.450 --> 01:17:51.220
So the superintendent of the colony basically needs an inmate

01:17:51.220 --> 01:17:53.500
that he can say, I'm gonna sterilize you,

01:17:53.900 --> 01:17:55.560
have that person challenge the law,

01:17:56.110 --> 01:17:57.970
and then hopefully prevail against her.

01:17:58.810 --> 01:17:59.890
So he's looking for someone.

01:18:00.060 --> 01:18:02.520
and Carrie Buck checks a lot of boxes.

01:18:05.230 --> 01:18:06.530
From the board's perspective,

01:18:06.980 --> 01:18:09.840
the menace posed by Buck's own feeble-mindedness

01:18:09.840 --> 01:18:11.440
was doubled by her lineage.

01:18:12.360 --> 01:18:15.360
Her mother, who was alleged to have engaged in prostitution,

01:18:15.880 --> 01:18:17.880
was likewise an inmate at the colony.

01:18:19.580 --> 01:18:22.200
By the laws of heredity, the board concluded,

01:18:23.320 --> 01:18:25.760
Carrie Buck is the probable potential parent

01:18:25.760 --> 01:18:28.020
of socially inadequate offspring.

01:18:29.360 --> 01:18:29.960
It was recommended...

01:18:30.000 --> 01:18:39.860
she be sterilized for both her own welfare and the good of society. Then Buck was assigned

01:18:39.860 --> 01:18:46.720
an attorney, friendly to the eugenic cause, who would appeal her sterilization, ideally

01:18:46.720 --> 01:18:54.220
all the way to the Supreme Court of the United States. Across the country, eugenicists would

01:18:54.220 --> 01:18:59.790
be watching to see if the Virginia test case could create a national consensus.

01:19:00.000 --> 01:19:01.330
on sterilization.

01:19:05.600 --> 01:19:08.490
Sterilization was a radical procedure.

01:19:10.220 --> 01:19:13.100
Between 1915 and the mid-1920s,

01:19:13.370 --> 01:19:16.500
you have a dozen or more states that passed laws

01:19:16.500 --> 01:19:19.340
that allowed for mandatory sterilization

01:19:19.490 --> 01:19:21.310
of people and institutions.

01:19:22.600 --> 01:19:24.140
Some of them were used actively.

01:19:24.830 --> 01:19:26.670
Many of them were just on the books,

01:19:26.710 --> 01:19:28.610
but nobody was being operated on.

01:19:28.850 --> 01:19:29.930
Some of them have been struck.

01:19:30.000 --> 01:19:37.080
down by state courts, so it wasn't at all clear what was going to happen to eugenic sterilization.

01:19:38.610 --> 01:19:44.000
There was a hope among eugenicists. If we could just get a case that goes up to the Supreme Court,

01:19:44.410 --> 01:19:49.850
one ruling from the Supreme Court, and suddenly we've got a national legal standard that eugenic

01:19:49.850 --> 01:19:55.010
sterilization is acceptable, so that became high on the wish list of the eugenics movement.

01:19:57.200 --> 01:19:59.940
No one was more interested in the Virginia test case.

01:20:00.000 --> 01:20:05.790
than Harry Laughlin, who had spent much of the previous decade promoting sterilization

01:20:05.790 --> 01:20:12.930
as a cheap, effective way to rid the nation of what he called the socially inadequate classes.

01:20:13.880 --> 01:20:19.370
Harry Laughlin believes that to really move the needle on the national genetic pool

01:20:19.370 --> 01:20:22.320
and really improve things, sterilization was the answer.

01:20:23.800 --> 01:20:26.020
Eugenical sterilization was Laughlin's life work.

01:20:26.530 --> 01:20:29.130
He published a book in 1922.

01:20:30.000 --> 01:20:32.000
compendium of every law that had been passed.

01:20:32.300 --> 01:20:33.900
of every case that had been brought.

01:20:34.444 --> 01:20:37.884
excruciating detail about the history of eugenical sterilization.

01:20:38.764 --> 01:20:39.264
And

01:20:39.532 --> 01:20:42.072
It became the Bible for people who wanted to pass.

01:20:42.316 --> 01:20:44.316
sterilization laws.

01:20:46.028 --> 01:20:52.468
It was only a matter of time before Laughlin was asked to serve as an expert witness in the case against Carrie Buck.

01:20:54.668 --> 01:20:56.828
and though he was unable to appear in person,

01:20:57.612 --> 01:20:59.212
He was more than happy to help.

01:21:00.700 --> 01:21:02.670
Laughlin never met Carrie Buck.

01:21:03.280 --> 01:21:05.780
Laughlin never traveled to Virginia to see her.

01:21:05.880 --> 01:21:11.590
His testimony was read into the record of the Carrie Buck case as a deposition.

01:21:11.810 --> 01:21:14.790
The Buck family, Laughlin argued.

01:21:15.000 --> 01:21:21.450
was mentally defective, members of what he described as the shiftless, ignorant,

01:21:21.610 --> 01:21:27.180
and worthless class of antisocial whites of the South. As such,

01:21:27.670 --> 01:21:29.970
Carrie was certainly likely to give birth to a new generation.

01:21:30.092 --> 01:21:31.452
to defective children.

01:21:32.204 --> 01:21:34.404
no doubt with her infant daughter Vivian.

01:21:34.892 --> 01:21:35.872
she already had.

01:21:37.804 --> 01:21:40.304
Laughlin's testimony proved persuasive.

01:21:41.324 --> 01:21:42.864
as the eugenicists hoped.

01:21:43.628 --> 01:21:44.788
First, the county judge.

01:21:45.196 --> 01:21:46.616
then the state Supreme Court.

01:21:46.956 --> 01:21:49.956
upheld Virginia's sterilization law.

01:21:52.492 --> 01:21:54.492
the next and final ruling.

01:21:54.764 --> 01:21:57.764
would come from the Supreme Court of the United States.

01:22:00.000 --> 01:22:03.610
Poor Carrie Buck, there's no weaker person, perhaps,

01:22:03.770 --> 01:22:05.750
who's ever come before the Supreme Court.

01:22:05.960 --> 01:22:11.980
She is poor, and she is alone, and her mother is an inmate,

01:22:12.040 --> 01:22:14.720
and she has a lawyer that's been chosen by her.

01:22:15.000 --> 01:22:19.940
to not represent her, and she's asking the font of justice

01:22:19.940 --> 01:22:24.380
in our society, don't let them forcibly operate on me

01:22:24.380 --> 01:22:29.860
so I can't have children, and they say, go ahead, sterilize.

01:22:30.000 --> 01:22:39.020
In May 1927, the court's majority opinion was rendered by the venerable Oliver Wendell Holmes,

01:22:39.280 --> 01:22:44.620
who at 86 was widely regarded as America's most brilliant legal mind.

01:22:46.850 --> 01:22:53.140
It is better for all the world, Holmes wrote, if instead of waiting to execute degenerate

01:22:53.140 --> 01:22:59.940
offspring for crime or to let them starve for their imbecility, society can prevent those who

01:23:00.000 --> 01:23:01.480
were manifestly unfit.

01:23:01.852 --> 01:23:03.852
from continuing their kind.

01:23:05.052 --> 01:23:07.112
three generations of imbeciles.

01:23:07.484 --> 01:23:09.484
are enough.

01:23:11.036 --> 01:23:14.876
Justice Holmes says if she is allowed to reproduce,

01:23:15.260 --> 01:23:18.500
or if the carry bucks of the world in general are allowed to reproduce.

01:23:19.036 --> 01:23:22.136
This will be deleterious to American society.

01:23:24.380 --> 01:23:27.320
And so therefore, the government has the authority

01:23:27.708 --> 01:23:29.708
to step in and in pursuit.

01:23:30.000 --> 01:23:33.200
of the greater public good to suppress

01:23:33.200 --> 01:23:35.060
her individual right to reproduce.

01:23:40.380 --> 01:23:45.040
Carrie Buck was sterilized on October 19th, 1927.

01:23:46.580 --> 01:23:47.900
Less than a month afterward,

01:23:48.670 --> 01:23:50.370
she was paroled from the colony.

01:23:52.580 --> 01:23:55.870
Thanks to Carrie Buck, a jubilant Laughlin declared,

01:23:56.670 --> 01:23:59.610
eugenical sterilizations experimental.

01:24:00.000 --> 01:24:08.470
period had come to an end. Over the two decades that preceded the Carrie Buck case, only about

01:24:08.470 --> 01:24:16.140
6,000 sterilizations had been performed nationwide. In the six years that followed it, as states

01:24:16.140 --> 01:24:22.360
across the country rushed to enact sterilization laws, that number would more than double.

01:24:24.510 --> 01:24:29.290
If you look back at all the sterilization laws passed, the easiest way to sum up...

01:24:30.190 --> 01:24:33.890
who their targets were is round up the usual suspects.

01:24:35.070 --> 01:24:37.430
You are generally going to be dealing with poor people,

01:24:38.460 --> 01:24:41.060
people who are part of a disfavored minority,

01:24:42.420 --> 01:24:45.900
people who were on private charity or public welfare,

01:24:47.210 --> 01:24:50.490
people who had disabilities, mental or physical,

01:24:51.580 --> 01:24:53.400
and people who were generally considered

01:24:53.400 --> 01:24:55.080
somehow on the margins of society.

01:25:15.290 --> 01:25:23.080
The conceit of eugenics was that scientists understood what traits were associated with

01:25:23.550 --> 01:25:26.550
health and well-being over the long term.

01:25:27.650 --> 01:25:29.970
But hereditary science in the early 20s...

01:25:30.000 --> 01:25:38.400
century was still emerging. Eugenics led the public discussion, promoted the

01:25:38.400 --> 01:25:43.930
science of human heredity in a time when hereditarian scientists were

01:25:43.930 --> 01:25:47.530
themselves developing their craft. And I think for a period of time they saw this

01:25:47.530 --> 01:25:53.620
as a positive development, society taking interest in the kind of science

01:25:53.620 --> 01:25:58.660
that they were doing. And then I think by the 20s there's a problem.

01:26:03.740 --> 01:26:12.020
It was the fall of 1926, and geneticist Herman J. Muller, a former Columbia University flyboy,

01:26:12.730 --> 01:26:15.890
was looking for ways to speed his experiment along.

01:26:16.770 --> 01:26:24.280
He was still working with flies, though now on his own at the University of Texas in Austin.

01:26:25.070 --> 01:26:29.400
So far, he'd been using the technique he'd learned in the flyroom.

01:26:30.000 --> 01:26:37.030
Thomas Hunt Morgan. Hunt for naturally arising mutations, then track them across generations.

01:26:38.240 --> 01:26:44.040
Breed a generation, peer at its members one by one through a jeweler's loop, repeat.

01:26:45.840 --> 01:26:48.480
But at this point, Muller had lost patience.

01:26:50.580 --> 01:26:55.500
It took an enormous amount of time to generate these mutants. You had to wait until you

01:26:55.500 --> 01:26:58.360
basically found one. It was a process of discovery.

01:27:00.320 --> 01:27:05.420
So Mueller began to wonder whether he could actually create mutants de novo from scratch

01:27:05.420 --> 01:27:09.260
by doing something to the genes.

01:27:11.480 --> 01:27:15.620
One night, on a whim, Mueller switched on the x-ray machine

01:27:15.620 --> 01:27:18.490
and began irradiating male fruit flies.

01:27:19.940 --> 01:27:23.040
Once they'd been exposed, he slid them into glass bottles

01:27:23.040 --> 01:27:25.580
with a roughly equal number of female flies.

01:27:26.330 --> 01:27:27.010
Then he waited.

01:27:30.000 --> 01:27:34.760
When the larvae began to appear on day five, it was clear the whim had worked.

01:27:37.170 --> 01:27:43.090
Mueller, by using the exact right dosage of x-rays, finds that he can make dozens of mutations,

01:27:43.560 --> 01:27:46.040
mutations that would have taken months or years to find.

01:27:46.060 --> 01:27:48.240
He becomes a mutant maker.

01:27:49.820 --> 01:27:56.940
He can't do it in a predictable way, but the principle that human gene material was

01:27:57.060 --> 01:27:59.970
was malleable, was changeable, is an idea that...

01:28:00.000 --> 01:28:07.450
understands and embraces." If an insect's genes could be altered by a blast of

01:28:07.450 --> 01:28:13.160
radiation, Muller realized, human genes one day might be manipulated as well.

01:28:14.730 --> 01:28:19.300
And heredity would no longer be the prerogative, he said, of an unreachable

01:28:19.300 --> 01:28:27.810
god playing pranks on us. The idea of controlling human heredity had

01:28:27.930 --> 01:28:29.830
captivated Muller since his earliest

01:28:30.000 --> 01:28:36.500
days in Morgan's lab. He'd been aware of the flaws in so-called eugenics science for nearly

01:28:36.500 --> 01:28:42.650
as long, and his doubts about the American eugenics movement had been steadily mounting.

01:28:45.530 --> 01:28:52.520
Mueller began to think that you couldn't have a eugenics movement without asking questions

01:28:52.520 --> 01:28:57.880
about equality. What was the criteria for judging a better human being than a worse human being

01:28:58.130 --> 01:28:59.890
and thereby sterilizing the

01:29:00.280 --> 01:29:02.700
the worst human being or selectively breeding the better human being.

01:29:04.450 --> 01:29:11.300
Who would ensure that the eugenics movement was selecting the best features

01:29:11.300 --> 01:29:15.280
when the best features were dictated by the elites?

01:29:21.950 --> 01:29:24.990
Concerns about the eugenics movement had been raised before,

01:29:25.070 --> 01:29:29.340
but they'd come mainly from lone voices shouting into the wind.

01:29:30.860 --> 01:29:35.360
Now, increasingly, hereditary scientists began to speak as one.

01:29:39.080 --> 01:29:45.060
More and more scientists are realizing that heredity is not something that you can understand

01:29:45.060 --> 01:29:49.220
simply like Mendel understood his pea plants.

01:29:50.130 --> 01:29:54.950
That some human traits are really complex, and you can't predict whether they're going

01:29:54.950 --> 01:29:56.310
to appear or reappear.

01:29:56.970 --> 01:29:59.970
that some conditions that we think of

01:30:00.000 --> 01:30:02.880
is hereditary are really about social issues.

01:30:04.240 --> 01:30:08.040
Nobody really discards the idea that heredity is important,

01:30:08.450 --> 01:30:12.210
but there is a growing chorus of scientists

01:30:12.860 --> 01:30:15.580
who are being more careful in the way

01:30:15.580 --> 01:30:16.940
that they talk about heredity.

01:30:18.900 --> 01:30:21.240
Even the father of the intelligence test,

01:30:21.460 --> 01:30:24.900
Henry Goddard, who had done so much to stoke fears

01:30:24.900 --> 01:30:26.880
of hereditary feeble-mindedness,

01:30:27.730 --> 01:30:29.650
disavowed his earlier conclusions.

01:30:30.830 --> 01:30:34.540
In particular, he regretted having coined the term moron.

01:30:35.600 --> 01:30:37.700
With proper education, he now believed,

01:30:38.170 --> 01:30:42.190
such individuals were perfectly capable of managing their own affairs.

01:30:43.720 --> 01:30:48.140
Eugenic scientists were doing what they understood to be reliable science,

01:30:48.140 --> 01:30:51.940
and it turned out that in many cases their science was mistaken.

01:30:52.760 --> 01:30:53.880
Science is a process.

01:30:54.870 --> 01:30:58.710
People make claims, they advance evidence for it,

01:30:59.040 --> 01:30:59.980
and then others come...

01:31:00.000 --> 01:31:04.810
along who have a more sophisticated understanding of the methodological

01:31:04.810 --> 01:31:11.860
problems and they say hey prostitution may result from a woman's having no

01:31:11.860 --> 01:31:19.240
other choice economically or alcoholism may arise from all sorts of stresses in

01:31:19.260 --> 01:31:28.870
one's life you don't need genetics at all to explain these things as the 1920s

01:31:28.870 --> 01:31:29.690
came to a close

01:31:30.510 --> 01:31:34.640
and the Great Depression radically rearranged American society,

01:31:35.640 --> 01:31:39.000
the dogma of the eugenics movement rang ever more hollow.

01:31:41.020 --> 01:31:42.760
25% of the country is unemployed.

01:31:43.440 --> 01:31:45.340
People's life savings have been wiped out

01:31:45.340 --> 01:31:48.020
by both the stock market crash and the bank failures.

01:31:48.650 --> 01:31:51.470
The person who's now on the breadline

01:31:51.850 --> 01:31:54.790
might have been a lawyer who graduated from Harvard.

01:31:54.790 --> 01:31:59.900
And this was a clear indication that poverty was not...

01:32:00.000 --> 01:32:10.120
biological when in 1932 yet another eugenics congress convened in New York

01:32:11.170 --> 01:32:17.220
most in the scientific community declined to attend they hold this

01:32:17.220 --> 01:32:24.570
conference to propagate the idea of eugenics all the same guys are there

01:32:24.570 --> 01:32:29.980
Madison Grant Charles Benedict Davenport Harry Laughlin espoused

01:32:30.000 --> 01:32:36.840
the same ideas. Their ideas have not changed in 25 years, and almost nobody comes. Because

01:32:36.840 --> 01:32:43.200
among scientists, eugenics is now viewed as the purview of a bunch of old white cranks

01:32:43.200 --> 01:32:45.620
whom science has passed by.

01:32:49.230 --> 01:32:55.660
Improbably, Herman Muller did turn up at the Congress, though only to deliver a scathing

01:32:55.660 --> 01:32:59.970
10-minute speech. There is no scientific basis.

01:33:00.000 --> 01:33:03.220
for the conclusion that the socially lower classes

01:33:03.220 --> 01:33:06.160
have genetically inferior intellectual equipment,

01:33:06.340 --> 01:33:06.920
he insisted.

01:33:08.400 --> 01:33:10.500
Certain slum districts of our cities

01:33:10.500 --> 01:33:14.330
are veritable factories for the production of criminality

01:33:14.420 --> 01:33:16.700
among those who happen to be born in them.

01:33:18.130 --> 01:33:20.780
Under these circumstances, it is society,

01:33:21.330 --> 01:33:23.810
not the individual which is the real criminal

01:33:24.380 --> 01:33:26.180
and which stands to be judged.

01:33:26.380 --> 01:33:29.970
The problem of eugenic thinking...

01:33:30.000 --> 01:33:36.500
was an utter ignorance of social causes of social problems,

01:33:37.190 --> 01:33:39.650
a tendency to over-biologize,

01:33:39.650 --> 01:33:44.390
to think through the biological lens about everything in society.

01:33:46.250 --> 01:33:50.050
Eugenics might yet perfect the human race, Mueller told the audience,

01:33:51.420 --> 01:33:55.320
but only in a society consciously organized for the common good.

01:34:07.900 --> 01:34:13.140
In July 1933, in Germany, Adolf Hitler came to power

01:34:13.140 --> 01:34:19.970
and immediately enshrined eugenics in state policy,

01:34:20.910 --> 01:34:24.610
with a law that mandated the sterilization of men and women

01:34:24.860 --> 01:34:26.980
suffering from any one of nine,

01:34:27.020 --> 01:34:28.860
presumably heritable conditions.

01:34:31.530 --> 01:34:35.630
It had been based on a model law written by Harry Laughlin.

01:34:38.280 --> 01:34:40.940
Before Hitler, there was a German eugenics movement,

01:34:41.250 --> 01:34:44.050
but it did not have a sterilization law.

01:34:44.760 --> 01:34:47.780
The sterilization law was ultimately enacted

01:34:47.780 --> 01:34:51.760
with the inspiration of what American states had been doing.

01:34:52.680 --> 01:34:59.880
Harry Laughlin is correspondent.

01:35:00.000 --> 01:35:04.640
with German scientists all along and encouraging them.

01:35:05.220 --> 01:35:10.100
He's proud of the fact that when the Nazis adopt eugenic sterilization law,

01:35:10.530 --> 01:35:12.370
it's strongly modeled on his own law.

01:35:13.750 --> 01:35:18.030
The United States has the reputation of being on the forefront of scientific endeavor.

01:35:19.080 --> 01:35:24.920
When Adolf Hitler was in prison, he read Madison Grant's The Passing of the Great Race,

01:35:26.190 --> 01:35:28.130
wrote Madison Grant a fan letter saying,

01:35:28.150 --> 01:35:29.390
This book is my Bible.

01:35:31.150 --> 01:35:33.730
And when he wrote Mein Kampf, his autobiography,

01:35:34.110 --> 01:35:38.980
he said we, Germans, must emulate what the Americans are doing.

01:35:42.220 --> 01:35:46.440
Nazi officials estimated no fewer than 400,000 Germans

01:35:46.440 --> 01:35:50.960
would be sterilized, roughly 25 times the number

01:35:50.960 --> 01:35:53.440
sterilized in the United States so far.

01:35:55.480 --> 01:35:57.660
The more zealous American eugenicists

01:35:57.710 --> 01:35:59.110
applauded the Nazi law.

01:36:00.000 --> 01:36:03.610
which apply to all people, whether institutionalized or not.

01:36:05.120 --> 01:36:07.520
As one Virginia sterilization advocate put it,

01:36:08.190 --> 01:36:10.630
the Germans are beating us at our own game.

01:36:14.100 --> 01:36:18.980
But for many Americans, the news from Germany was accompanied by an uncomfortable revelation.

01:36:20.860 --> 01:36:25.380
Many interviewed about the Hitler proposal expressed shock, the Daily News reported.

01:36:26.390 --> 01:36:29.970
They were surprised to find out that 27 of our 48 Americans

01:36:30.000 --> 01:36:33.300
have laws permitting the performance of sterilization

01:36:33.740 --> 01:36:35.490
upon the feeble-minded.

01:36:38.590 --> 01:36:43.560
The 1930s was the peak of eugenic sterilization,

01:36:45.070 --> 01:36:50.330
and that was after geneticists, professional scientific geneticists,

01:36:50.560 --> 01:36:54.360
had largely abandoned the eugenic program.

01:36:56.160 --> 01:36:59.970
There's this trend that discredits the doctrine on...

01:37:00.000 --> 01:37:01.900
on which eugenic sterilization is based.

01:37:02.260 --> 01:37:04.540
At the same time, paradoxically,

01:37:05.360 --> 01:37:09.240
sterilization rates shot up in the United States

01:37:09.800 --> 01:37:11.120
because of the depression.

01:37:12.350 --> 01:37:15.770
It costs money to keep people in homes for the feeble-minded.

01:37:16.720 --> 01:37:20.020
So if you want to reduce the cost of keeping people,

01:37:20.560 --> 01:37:24.460
you sterilize them, and that's what happened.

01:37:27.410 --> 01:37:29.840
Just what sterilization is, so would I.

01:37:30.380 --> 01:37:31.280
How do they do it?

01:37:31.650 --> 01:37:32.650
Well, I'll tell you.

01:37:33.200 --> 01:37:37.790
As public awareness of eugenic sterilization spread,

01:37:38.940 --> 01:37:41.320
controversial Hollywood film opened in theaters,

01:37:43.460 --> 01:37:47.260
cautionary tale about good intentions gone dangerously wrong.

01:37:47.780 --> 01:37:50.580
And to stop me from having children ever?

01:37:52.010 --> 01:37:52.570
Exactly.

01:37:53.800 --> 01:37:55.420
Released in 1934,

01:37:56.330 --> 01:37:59.970
Tomorrow's Children told the story of 17-year-old Alice Mason.

01:38:00.000 --> 01:38:05.840
the sole functional member of an otherwise drunken, crippled, feeble-minded family

01:38:05.840 --> 01:38:10.380
who was slated for sterilization along with her parents and siblings.

01:38:10.800 --> 01:38:12.980
Three generations of unfit are enough.

01:38:13.630 --> 01:38:18.170
And saved from the scalpel only by the revelation that she'd been adopted.

01:38:18.630 --> 01:38:24.080
Look at me, that I'm well and strong, and I'll be as good as to judge.

01:38:27.400 --> 01:38:29.980
Tomorrow's Children raises the question of whether or not you...

01:38:30.030 --> 01:38:31.990
always get it right when you sterilize someone.

01:38:33.070 --> 01:38:35.410
How much can you really know about someone's background?

01:38:36.060 --> 01:38:38.620
Without getting into the details of

01:38:38.620 --> 01:38:42.300
how much do we understand about genetics in 1934,

01:38:42.780 --> 01:38:46.180
it simply says sometimes people make mistakes

01:38:46.180 --> 01:38:49.100
with these things, and so maybe we should be more careful.

01:38:52.730 --> 01:38:54.950
Tomorrow's Children was still playing on screens

01:38:54.950 --> 01:38:59.780
across the country when, in 1935, a committee of scientists

01:39:00.250 --> 01:39:07.050
turned up at Cold Spring Harbor. They'd been sent by the Carnegie Institution, which had sponsored

01:39:07.050 --> 01:39:12.910
the Eugenics Record Office since 1918 and had long been embarrassed by its political activities.

01:39:15.000 --> 01:39:21.500
Now Carnegie's board of directors had ordered a review of the work being done there. The visiting

01:39:21.500 --> 01:39:27.740
committee's report was decidedly unfavorable. From a scientific vantage, they concluded,

01:39:28.290 --> 01:39:29.970
the thousands of heredity records

01:39:30.000 --> 01:39:35.200
stored in the famed fireproof vault were useless for the study of human genetics.

01:39:38.260 --> 01:39:43.890
They rightly saw that this eugenics fieldwork was largely ridiculous and was not scientific,

01:39:43.890 --> 01:39:49.390
but they also were troubled by the degree to which clearly Harry Laughlin was acting

01:39:49.390 --> 01:39:53.180
not as a scientist but as an evangelist for eugenics.

01:39:54.070 --> 01:39:58.980
And this was a clear indication that the tide was really turning against eugenics.

01:40:00.990 --> 01:40:03.710
For the movement's faithful, the message was plain.

01:40:04.700 --> 01:40:07.340
If they were going to continue to cull the unfit,

01:40:08.120 --> 01:40:10.460
they would need a new justification for it.

01:40:21.200 --> 01:40:24.460
From the moment the case of the sterilized heiress first hit the news,

01:40:24.940 --> 01:40:29.900
in January 1936, Americans were enthralled by it.

01:40:31.400 --> 01:40:38.180
First, there was the girl, Ann Cooper Hewitt, a San Francisco socialite who stood to inherit

01:40:38.180 --> 01:40:44.530
two-thirds of her late father's vast estate, and her shocking claim that her mother had

01:40:44.530 --> 01:40:47.660
had her sterilized to gain control of that inheritance.

01:40:49.700 --> 01:40:55.720
Ann Cooper Hewitt is sent to the hospital for an emergency appendectomy, and she comes

01:40:55.720 --> 01:40:56.900
out sterilized.

01:40:57.470 --> 01:40:59.150
And when she discovers it...

01:41:00.000 --> 01:41:06.000
She is understandably horrified, and she sues both her mother and the two surgeons.

01:41:07.090 --> 01:41:11.700
She claims that her mother has done it because her father's.

01:41:11.860 --> 01:41:18.880
Will stipulates that if Anne should die childless, the inheritance would go to her mother.

01:41:21.520 --> 01:41:27.130
Equally intriguing was the claim of the mother, Marion, that her daughter Anne was feeble-minded,

01:41:28.090 --> 01:41:29.970
a diagnosis based on an...

01:41:30.000 --> 01:41:37.590
test she'd been given just hours before her sterilization and says that she's

01:41:37.590 --> 01:41:42.480
writhing in pain and then a woman walks in the room and the woman starts asking

01:41:42.480 --> 01:41:47.240
her all these questions what's the longest river in the United States and

01:41:47.400 --> 01:41:53.850
how many years is a presidential term and Ann's reaction is why are you asking

01:41:53.850 --> 01:41:59.250
me these asinine questions what does this have to do with appendicitis and she

01:41:59.250 --> 01:41:59.850
doesn't answer

01:42:00.000 --> 01:42:00.760
of the questions.

01:42:02.530 --> 01:42:05.930
Although her score identified the girl as a high-grade moron,

01:42:06.610 --> 01:42:09.410
a court-appointed psychiatrist at a preliminary hearing

01:42:09.410 --> 01:42:13.200
found her to be well-read, fluent in French and Italian,

01:42:13.940 --> 01:42:16.080
and perfectly normal in every respect.

01:42:18.420 --> 01:42:20.540
The Cooper Hewitt sterilization case

01:42:20.540 --> 01:42:22.490
was one of those cases that people

01:42:22.490 --> 01:42:23.630
call the trial of the century.

01:42:24.500 --> 01:42:26.020
Headlines all over the country.

01:42:26.900 --> 01:42:28.600
And if you weren't paying attention

01:42:28.600 --> 01:42:29.980
to what sterilization was,

01:42:30.000 --> 01:42:33.080
was by then you would have heard in that story.

01:42:35.610 --> 01:42:38.090
Ann Cooper Hewitt is not emblematic

01:42:38.090 --> 01:42:40.970
of the typical sterilization patient.

01:42:41.750 --> 01:42:45.420
And for that very reason, she gets a lot more attention.

01:42:47.520 --> 01:42:48.700
By eugenics standards,

01:42:48.980 --> 01:42:51.200
Ann was the very definition of well-born.

01:42:52.320 --> 01:42:56.380
She was the scion of the successful, white, wealthy,

01:42:57.040 --> 01:42:59.700
seemingly sound in both body and mind.

01:43:01.050 --> 01:43:06.400
On what grounds then, those following the case may well have wondered, could her sterilization

01:43:06.400 --> 01:43:07.860
possibly be justified?

01:43:10.330 --> 01:43:16.070
Attorney I.M. Golden, who represented the surgeons named in the suit, wondered much

01:43:16.070 --> 01:43:20.460
the same, and he decided to solicit the opinion of an expert.

01:43:22.540 --> 01:43:28.200
In May 1936, he composed a letter to one of California's leading eugenicists, Paul

01:43:28.200 --> 01:43:29.980
Poppeno, and later...

01:43:30.000 --> 01:43:35.940
out for him the details of the case. Among them, the reasons Marion Cooper-Hewitt had given for

01:43:36.060 --> 01:43:45.140
wanting her daughter sterilized. Marion makes three charges about her daughter's behavior

01:43:45.140 --> 01:43:52.760
that she sees as indicative of someone who is mentally defective. The first is that she

01:43:52.760 --> 01:43:59.940
becomes infatuated with a chauffeur. The second is that she is infatuated with men in uniform.

01:44:00.060 --> 01:44:07.300
And then finally, that she has plans to run off with a Negro porter on a train.

01:44:08.300 --> 01:44:14.230
These are not people that probably Marianne believed her daughter should be associating with.

01:44:14.950 --> 01:44:16.950
Not somebody she should have children with.

01:44:20.340 --> 01:44:24.510
In your opinion, Golden asked Papineau, was it proper to sterilize her?

01:44:24.750 --> 01:44:27.110
As a matter of medical and scientific procedure?

01:44:29.180 --> 01:44:29.960
Paul Pau.

01:44:30.000 --> 01:44:33.180
Papineau long had been a proponent of eugenic sterilization.

01:44:34.340 --> 01:44:37.470
But the argument that an immoral, over-sexed girl

01:44:37.470 --> 01:44:39.730
would pass on those traits genetically

01:44:39.730 --> 01:44:42.340
could no longer plausibly be made.

01:44:43.840 --> 01:44:45.940
So Papineau offered another rationale,

01:44:47.260 --> 01:44:49.000
one that had been recently formulated

01:44:49.000 --> 01:44:52.020
and recommended by the American Eugenics Society.

01:44:54.540 --> 01:44:56.300
Heredity, Papineau told Golden,

01:44:56.740 --> 01:44:58.920
is not particularly the issue in this case.

01:45:00.350 --> 01:45:03.090
But I suppose we should all answer negatively the question

01:45:03.240 --> 01:45:07.820
whether a young woman such as you describe would be a desirable mother.

01:45:10.200 --> 01:45:15.190
In the 30s, the eugenic rationale for sterilization

01:45:15.370 --> 01:45:18.830
begins to morph into a kind of more generalized understanding

01:45:18.830 --> 01:45:20.990
that this person isn't fit to be a parent.

01:45:22.520 --> 01:45:25.960
That turns the whole argument about eugenics on its head

01:45:26.530 --> 01:45:29.970
because the determining question was not, will she spread her j-

01:45:30.000 --> 01:45:33.740
genetic defect, but will she make a desirable mother?

01:45:34.880 --> 01:45:40.230
When the trial of the two surgeons

01:45:40.230 --> 01:45:41.890
got underway in San Francisco,

01:45:42.800 --> 01:45:44.940
Anne's questionable capacity to mother

01:45:44.940 --> 01:45:46.940
was the centerpiece of the defense.

01:45:48.500 --> 01:45:51.440
The girl's sexual behavior alone, Golden argued,

01:45:52.140 --> 01:45:55.320
cast grave doubt on her ability to provide good moral

01:45:55.320 --> 01:45:57.870
and intellectual training to her offspring.

01:46:00.730 --> 01:46:06.890
In the end, the argument had little effect on the judge who, after six days of listening

01:46:06.890 --> 01:46:12.870
to testimony, abruptly called a halt to the proceedings and dismissed the case on the

01:46:12.870 --> 01:46:15.950
grounds that sterilization was legal in California.

01:46:19.020 --> 01:46:25.760
But in the public mind, sterilization had been effectively recast as a preventative

01:46:25.760 --> 01:46:27.780
measure against inept parenting.

01:46:30.320 --> 01:46:32.600
This is not a story that happens in an institution.

01:46:32.930 --> 01:46:34.890
It's a story about a socialite.

01:46:35.710 --> 01:46:39.390
Nevertheless, the same themes of needing to sterilize people

01:46:39.390 --> 01:46:41.680
for their own good come up.

01:46:43.040 --> 01:46:44.000
Forget about heredity.

01:46:44.480 --> 01:46:47.190
These people will be unable to take care of their children,

01:46:47.230 --> 01:46:50.460
so the humane thing to do is not let them have any.

01:46:52.540 --> 01:46:54.950
Eugenics simply becomes part of the machinery

01:46:54.950 --> 01:46:57.780
of how these state institutions function.

01:46:58.970 --> 01:46:59.970
Hereditary defect is...

01:47:00.000 --> 01:47:05.530
It's no longer part of the conversation, and it's simply a question of a state attempting

01:47:05.530 --> 01:47:12.260
to use all the tools available to limit the number of people who are seen to be a social

01:47:12.260 --> 01:47:13.620
and economic burden.

01:47:15.660 --> 01:47:21.880
By the close of the 1930s, more than 30,000 Americans had been sterilized nationwide.

01:47:31.530 --> 01:47:37.360
I think eugenics appeals to some real strong elements in the human psyche.

01:47:39.170 --> 01:47:46.760
One part of that, the positive part, is that there is a desire among people to perfect things.

01:47:48.180 --> 01:47:53.870
The negative side is we're also a species that is very prone to tribalism.

01:47:54.050 --> 01:47:59.970
We're very prone to believe that, you know, our people are the right people and other people.

01:48:00.870 --> 01:48:24.840
For a time, the enemies within American society were eclipsed by those without, and the nation's attention diverted by a conflict that consumed much of the world.

01:48:28.340 --> 01:48:29.960
Then came the liberation of...

01:48:30.000 --> 01:48:38.550
Buchenwald, and Dachau, and the chilling evidence of eugenic policies carried to a monstrous

01:48:38.550 --> 01:48:39.010
extreme.

01:48:41.940 --> 01:48:46.780
By the mid-1940s, the full horror of what happens in Nazi Germany becomes apparent.

01:48:48.860 --> 01:48:56.040
The movement from sterilization to extermination, the killing of several millions based on this

01:48:56.040 --> 01:48:59.960
kind of idea of the betterment of human race, and it creates a vast...

01:49:00.000 --> 01:49:08.120
embarrassment for the American eugenics movement. People were repelled and began

01:49:08.120 --> 01:49:18.590
to turn away from eugenics and eugenics became a dirty word. The Holocaust being

01:49:19.090 --> 01:49:26.940
tied to a wide range of eugenic practices is a blemish on humans as a species

01:49:26.940 --> 01:49:29.960
and it under...

01:49:30.000 --> 01:49:35.360
any notion that eugenics was a positive force in American society.

01:49:36.210 --> 01:49:42.440
Surgical sterilization was thought to be too slow and too expensive to be used on a mass scale.

01:49:43.330 --> 01:49:49.190
After the war, when the Allies put the Nazis on trial at Nuremberg,

01:49:49.550 --> 01:49:52.540
one of the charges was eugenic sterilization,

01:49:52.920 --> 01:49:56.410
and the lawyer for the Nazi who was charged said,

01:49:56.530 --> 01:49:59.560
you know, how can you charge my client with...

01:50:00.060 --> 01:50:05.910
the crime of eugenic sterilization when your own U.S. Supreme Court said this was okay.

01:50:12.190 --> 01:50:18.480
By the end of the 1940s, the eugenics movement had faded from the mainstream of American life.

01:50:20.290 --> 01:50:25.370
But the laws that had been passed in the name of eugenics would remain on the books for decades,

01:50:26.810 --> 01:50:29.790
barring some people from entering the country and others.

01:50:30.090 --> 01:50:36.570
from marriage and subjecting thousands to forced sterilization at the hands of the state.

01:50:38.940 --> 01:50:45.660
By the time such practices finally came to an end in the 1970s, the total number of sterilized

01:50:45.660 --> 01:50:55.600
Americans would exceed 60,000, and no matter the cost or the casualties, the scientific

01:50:55.600 --> 01:50:59.660
betterment of humanity would remain an irresistible aspiration.

01:51:01.470 --> 01:51:05.600
tempting generations to come with the promise of perfection.

01:51:09.420 --> 01:51:11.220
We believe in science.

01:51:12.140 --> 01:51:16.420
We want science to solve social problems.

01:51:17.800 --> 01:51:20.640
And we want to make ourselves better.

01:51:20.820 --> 01:51:23.400
I think everybody wants to do that.

01:51:27.040 --> 01:51:29.980
There is this idea that remains a kind of...

01:51:30.000 --> 01:51:36.440
hope that if we just get the science right, if only the right people are put

01:51:36.440 --> 01:51:46.590
in charge, that we can engineer our way to a better world. Some of the greatest

01:51:46.590 --> 01:51:51.280
social changes that have ever been accomplished have occurred because people

01:51:51.560 --> 01:51:56.260
were really willing to imagine impossible things, but the future that

01:51:56.260 --> 01:51:59.060
American eugenicists imagined was

01:52:00.000 --> 01:52:02.000
only a future for some.

01:52:04.540 --> 01:52:06.300
And so now the debate is,

01:52:06.684 --> 01:52:08.924
Are we going to use technology?

01:52:09.468 --> 01:52:14.748
to try to fulfill Galton's dream, if you will, of taking charge of our own evolution.

01:52:16.092 --> 01:52:17.432
And of course, it was a pipe dream.

01:52:18.428 --> 01:52:19.068
But nevertheless,

01:52:19.772 --> 01:52:21.772
It is a dream that persists.

01:52:23.772 --> 01:52:26.112
We have reason to be apprehensive.

01:52:26.172 --> 01:52:27.172
about this.

01:52:28.188 --> 01:52:29.288
And the...

01:52:29.500 --> 01:52:30.000
test tube.

01:52:30.000 --> 01:52:30.800
bears watching.
